  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Shower and thunderstorm chances shift southward\nwith a cold front through Friday, which will be followed by \ncooler temperatures.\n\nA few showers are noted in spots across parts of northeastern\nWest Virginia. While these showers should come and go over the\nnext hour or two, we will be watching two linear segments of\nshowers and embedded thunderstorms in southwestern and\nsoutheastern Pennsylvania. These segments have lost some of\ntheir lightning activity, but remain in tact enough that if they\nmove into Maryland in an hour or two, they could bring brief\ngusty winds and downpours. The thunderstorms are not severe.\nThis activity that we are watching are along a cold front that\nwill continue to push southward throughout the night and during\nthe day Friday. Some patchy fog could occur particularly in\nareas that have received rainfall this past afternoon and early\nevening. \n\nWhile the wind shift portion of the cold front likely pushes\nsouth of the area Friday morning, the moisture gradient may hang\nback across central Virginia up to the Potomac Highlands. As a \nshortwave trough swings through during the afternoon, some \nadditional showers and thunderstorms may develop in these areas.\nTemperatures will fall back into the lower to mid 80s with\nnortherly gusts up to 20 mph at times. There may also be a fair\namount of stratocumulus at times as well. A cooler night lies \nahead as high pressure crosses southern Quebec into New England.\nFriday night's lows should be in the upper 50s to mid 60s. \n\nHigh pressure to the northeast on Saturday should provide enough\ndry air to keep rain at bay. One exception may be along the\nAllegheny Front due to convergence in an inverted surface\ntrough. Warm advection aloft and mid level perturbations will \nlikely provide cloud cover areawide. Temperatures will be \ncloser to climatology, ranging from the mid 70s to lower 80s.\nLow pressure moving into the Ohio Valley may increase rain\nchances Saturday night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms return at times Sunday\ninto next week, with temperatures likely falling below normal.\n\nA cold front drops through the forecast area on Sunday before \nstalling to the south, bringing daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. In addition to daily precipitation, a cooler airmass \nbuilds overhead in the wake of the cold front, yielding cooling \ntemperatures each day. On Sunday, high temperatures will be in the \nlow 80s for most (70s mtns) with overnight lows in the 60s. By \nmidweek, high temperatures will top out in the upper 50s to 60s with \novernight lows in the 50s. These temperatures remain somewhat\nuncertain with large model spread, but the forecast favors the\ncooler side of guidance given a cold air damming surface\npattern. Overall, nearby disturbances bring unsettled weather \nto the region beginning Sunday and persisting through midweek.",
